{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>If a man gives to his neighbor silver or vessels to keep</b> — This section is spoken of an unpaid bailee, and therefore Scripture exempts him for theft, according to the tradition of our Sages (Mekhilta here; Bava Metzia 94a). Scripture mentions it without qualification because the way of those who guard silver or vessels is to guard them without payment. The second section (below, verses 9–12) deals with a paid bailee; it mentions a donkey, an ox, a sheep, or any beast, for the way of animals is to give them into the hands of shepherds to guard and tend them for hire." "<b>If it is stolen from the man's house</b> — Rashi explained, according to his own reasoning, that this means he makes such a claim. And they brought him parallels: \"If a prophet arises among you\" (Deuteronomy 13:2), \"Hananiah son of Azzur the prophet\" (Jeremiah 28:1), for Scripture would not call him by this name in truth, but only that he says so. But there is no need for this, for Scripture says: if it is stolen from the man's house and the thief is found, he shall pay double; and if the thief is not found, they shall come to the court and he shall swear regarding the theft, that he has not laid his hand upon his neighbor's property; and whomsoever the court convict regarding this theft — that he is the thief — he shall pay double. For the court would not convict him to pay double unless he himself stole it, since the obligation of double payment applies to the thief, as it says above (Exodus 22:3), \"he shall pay double.\""], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {1 "Rashi's comment (on this verse) reads וגנב מבית האיש as meaning the bailee claims 'it was stolen from my house,' i.e., Scripture narrates his claim rather than stating fact. The parallels Rashi cites are cases where Scripture uses a title descriptively rather than affirming its truth."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:14:00.484954412Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}